# Types defined in the file's type prelude are in scope for every expression
# below; the comma between two definitions is optional
Ints = [Int]
Person = { name: String, age: Int },
People = [Person]
Adult = Int(i | i >= 18)

# A prelude type is used like any other: through the pipe operator
assert((['1', '2', '3'] |> Ints) == [1, 2, 3])
assert(({ name: 'Alice', age: '30' } |> Person) == { name: 'Alice', age: 30 })
# A prelude type can refer to one defined before it
assert(([{ name: 'Alice', age: '30' }] |> People) == [{ name: 'Alice', age: 30 }])
assert(sum(['1', '2'] |> Ints) == 3)
# Constraints are enforced, as in a let type definition
assert(('18' |> Adult) == 18)
assertFails(fn( ~> '17' |> Adult ))
assertFails(fn( ~> ['1', 'bad'] |> Ints ))
assertFails(fn( ~> { name: 'Alice' } |> Person ))
# Prelude types are visible inside nested expressions
assert((let xs = ['1', '2'] |> Ints in sum(xs)) == 3)
assert(map(['1', '2'], fn( x ~> [x] |> Ints )) == [[1], [2]])
